<header class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h1>Terms of service</h1>
        <p>The terms on which institutions and their users use Gadfly Exams.</p>
    </div>
</header>

<div class="wrap prose">
    <p class="updated">Last updated 21 November 2026.</p>

    <h2 id="who">1. Who these terms are between</h2>
    <p>Gadfly Exams ("the service") is provided by MindAttic ("we", "us"). An institution, company or other body that
        signs up ("the customer") agrees to these terms for itself and is responsible for the people it gives accounts to
        ("users"): its staff and students. Where the customer has signed an order form or agreement with us, that document
        takes priority over these terms if they conflict.</p>

    <h2 id="service">2. The service</h2>
    <p>The service lets the customer upload course material, build spoken examinations from it, have students take
        them with an AI examiner, and grade, review and release the results. Features change over time; we will not remove
        a feature the customer relies on during a paid term without telling them in advance.</p>

    <h2 id="ai">3. AI-assisted assessment</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Grades produced by the AI panel are preliminary recommendations. A grade reaches a student only after a person
            authorized by the customer approves it. The customer remains responsible for the grades it releases.</li>
        <li>Integrity notes (for example, a face out of view, a second voice or a change of browser tab) are
            low-confidence observations for a human reviewer. They are not findings of misconduct and must not be used as
            the only basis for one.</li>
        <li>We make no claim that the service measures understanding more validly than other forms of assessment until our
            pilot studies measure it. The customer decides whether the service suits each use.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="accounts">4. Accounts</h2>
    <p>Users must keep their sign-in details to themselves and use two-step verification where the service requires it.
        The customer manages who has an account and what role each user has, and must remove accounts that should no
        longer have access. Tell us promptly if you believe an account has been misused.</p>

    <h2 id="customer">5. The customer's responsibilities</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Have the right to upload the course material it uses, and a lawful basis to have its students examined and
            their exam data processed.</li>
        <li>Tell students how the service is used in their assessment, and offer appropriate alternatives or
            accommodations to students who need them.</li>
        <li>Configure retention, regional settings and integrity levels to match its own policies and the law that applies to it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="use">6. Acceptable use</h2>
    <p>Users must not: try to reach another organization's data; probe, overload or disrupt the service; get around rate
        limits, sign-in or access controls; reverse-engineer the service except where the law allows it; upload material
        that is unlawful or infringes someone else's rights; or use the service to harass or discriminate against anyone.
        We may suspend an account that does any of these, and will tell the customer why.</p>

    <h2 id="content">7. Content and data</h2>
    <p>The customer keeps all rights in the material it uploads and in its users' answers and results. It gives us
        permission to store and process them only to provide the service, as described in the <a href="privacy">privacy
        notice</a> and any data processing agreement between us. Students' answers are sent to the AI providers that run
        the examiner and the scoring panel for that purpose, never with video or account details.</p>

    <h2 id="fees">8. Fees</h2>
    <p>Fees, seats, exam allowances and the term are set in the customer's order form. Trials are free for the period
        stated and end automatically.</p>

    <h2 id="availability">9. Availability and support</h2>
    <p>We work to keep the service available and to fix problems quickly, but unless an order form says otherwise we do
        not promise a particular level of availability. We may carry out maintenance, and will try to schedule it outside
        exam periods the customer tells us about.</p>

    <h2 id="ending">10. Ending the agreement</h2>
    <p>Either side may end the agreement as the order form allows. Before it ends, the customer can export its data,
        including each student's records. After it ends we delete the customer's data within the period set in the data
        processing agreement, except where the law requires us to keep something.</p>

    <h2 id="warranty">11. Warranties and liability</h2>
    <p>Except as the order form says, the service is provided as it is. Neither side limits liability that cannot be
        limited by law. Otherwise each side's total liability under these terms is limited as set in the order form.</p>

    <h2 id="changes">12. Changes to these terms</h2>
    <p>We may update these terms. We will tell customers about material changes before they take effect; continuing to
        use the service after that means accepting them.</p>

    <h2 id="contact">13. Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions about these terms: use the <a href="contact">contact form</a> and choose "Something else".</p>
</div>
